NNOOTTEESS
       When  ffoorrkk(2)  is  called  in a multithreaded process, only the calling
       thread is duplicated in the child process.  The original  intention  of
       pptthhrreeaadd__aattffoorrkk()  was  to  allow  the child process to be returned to a
       consistent state.  For example, at the time of  the  call  to  ffoorrkk(2),
       other  threads  may  have  locked mutexes that are visible in the user‐
       space memory duplicated in the child.  Such mutexes would never be  un‐
       locked,  since  the threads that placed the locks are not duplicated in
       the child.  The intent of pptthhrreeaadd__aattffoorrkk() was to provide  a  mechanism
       whereby  the  application  (or a library) could ensure that mutexes and
       other process and thread state would be restored to a consistent state.
       In practice, this task is generally too difficult to be practicable.

       After a ffoorrkk(2) in a multithreaded process returns in  the  child,  the
       child   should   call   only   async‐signal‐safe  functions  (see  ssiigg‐‐
       nnaall--ssaaffeettyy(7)) until such time as it calls eexxeeccvvee(2) to execute  a  new
       program.

       POSIX.1  specifies  that pptthhrreeaadd__aattffoorrkk() shall not fail with the error
       EEIINNTTRR.

